1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,211
Dilution happens when a
company issues new shares or

2
00:00:03,211 --> 00:00:06,792
securities that convert into
shares, so an existing holder

3
00:00:06,792 --> 00:00:09,200
owns a smaller percentage of the
total.

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,150
It does not automatically mean
the company is worth less.

5
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:18,112
Imagine you own 100 shares out
of 1,000, or 10%.

6
00:00:18,112 --> 00:00:23,018
If the company later issues 500
new shares and you receive none,

7
00:00:23,018 --> 00:00:27,387
you still hold 100 shares, but now
there are 1,500 total.

8
00:00:27,388 --> 00:00:31,575
Your percentage becomes about
6.67%.

9
00:00:31,575 --> 00:00:35,018
Those new shares might help
fund growth, but the outcome

10
00:00:35,018 --> 00:00:36,678
depends on how the money is

11
00:00:36,678 --> 00:00:39,138
used and what the company
becomes worth.

12
00:00:39,138 --> 00:00:42,769
SAFEs, convertible notes,
employee options, and new

13
00:00:42,769 --> 00:00:46,188
financing rounds can all affect the
share count.

14
00:00:46,188 --> 00:00:48,215
Some preferred shares may carry

15
00:00:48,215 --> 00:00:50,700
rights that differ from common
shares.

16
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:54,746
When reviewing an offering, look
at the current capitalization table

17
00:00:54,746 --> 00:00:58,138
and ask what securities could
convert or be issued later.

18
00:00:58,138 --> 00:00:59,895
A percentage today is not

19
00:00:59,895 --> 00:01:03,480
necessarily your percentage after
future financing.

